{"duration_ms":0,"id":22,"kind":"llm","model":"claude-opus-5","name":"claude-code.turn.14","provider":"anthropic","response":{"content":[{"text":"Fixed. The one-character change is in `stats.py:3`:\n\n```python\nreturn sum(values) / len(values)   # was //\n```\n\n`//` floors toward zero, so every mean under 1 collapsed to `0` (and `183.33` was being reported as `183`). True division matches the docstring's \"arithmetic mean\" contract.\n\nTwo things worth your attention, both consequences of `mean` being shared:\n\n**1. `billing.py` behavior changed too.** `average_invoice([10, 11])` now returns `10.5` instead of `10`. That's more correct, but if anything downstream assumes an `int` (formatting, cent-denominated math, a DB column, an equality test), it'll now see a float. I left it fixed rather than special-casing billing, since silently truncating invoice averages is the same bug — but if billing genuinely needs whole units, the right move is an explicit round at that call site, not a floor inside `mean`.\n\n**2. A unit question I can't resolve from this code.** For sub-second samples to have displayed as `0`, the values reaching `average_response_ms` must be *fractional seconds* — sub-second values expressed in ms (e.g. `400`) would have averaged to `400`, not `0`. If that's right, the function is returning seconds while its name promises milliseconds, and post-fix your dashboard will read `0.32 ms` where it should read `317 ms` — still wrong, just visibly so. There's no caller in this directory to confirm the unit, so I didn't guess at a `* 1000`. Tell me what feeds `samples` and I'll finish that half.","type":"text"}],"id":"msg_011Ceu825jwbc3yVE7LsPPgi","model":"claude-opus-5","role":"assistant","stop_reason":null,"usage":{"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":344,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"cache_creation_input_tokens":344,"cache_read_input_tokens":29095,"inference_geo":"not_available","input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard"}},"